WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.940
These action stars had major roles in really
big pictures during the genre's heyday in

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.620
the '80s and '90s, but they all ended their
career with a big question mark.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.200
Wondering whatever happened to these well-muscled
men and women?

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.960
We've got the answers you've been looking
for.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.320
Vernon Wells personified a very specific type
of 1980s villain: the guy who always seemed

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.740
to have his wardrobe on loan from an adult
club.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.290
It started with his role in Mad Max: Road
Warrior, in which he played shoulder-padded,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.660
Mohawked madman Wez.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.200
Wells also appeared as "Ransik," a recurring
villain in Power Rangers: Time Force in a

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.320
role that seemed cut from the same cloth as
Wez.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:39.860
"If I can't rule the present, then I'll just
rule the past!"

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.700
His other prominent role was as the over-the-top
villain Bennett in Commando.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.740
"Oh John, I feel good.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:47.760
Just like old times.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.060
What's it feel like to be a dying man?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.980
You're a dead man, John!"

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.380
These days, Wells looks more like a dad than
a leather daddy, and he has a sense of humor

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.150
about his characters becoming gay icons, but
to this day still swears there was no sexual

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:03.320
subtext intended.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.331
These days, Wells has been keeping himself
busy with bit parts and voice acting, but

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.300
mostly can be found focusing his career behind
the camera, directing and producing.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.140
He also works with Wolf Connection, a nature
preserve to domesticate wolves and allow bonding

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.000
with visitors and campers.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.460
In Aliens, Jenette Goldstein made a name for
herself by playing the tough, no-nonsense

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:22.960
Vasquez.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.560
"Hey Vasquez, have you ever been mistaken
for a man?"

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.220
"No.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.660
Have you?"

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.619
She also had roles as Meagan Shapiro in Lethal
Weapon 2 and the foster mother of John Connor

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.300
in Terminator 2: Judgment Day.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.960
Goldstein remains extremely proud of her genre-and-gender-defying
role, for good reason.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.740
Now she owns a company, Jenette Bras, named
after herself, naturally.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.310
She founded the company when she realized
department store lingerie departments were

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:53.060
rarely accommodating for women with smaller
band-sizes combined with larger cup sizes.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.580
Probably best known for playing the immortal
swordsman Connor MacLeod in cult 1986 hit

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.760
Highlander, Christopher Lambert seemed destined
for action movie stardom.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.940
"There can be only one."

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.360
But while he popped up in a handful of other
action properties in the '90s, he couldn't

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.989
escape the shadow of the Highlander.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.280
When the big offers stopped coming and B-movies
loomed, he decided to branch out with some

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.160
new business ventures, including a winery
in Provence.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.920
Apparently, they all took off.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.200
Still, he occasionally appears in modern action
movies, like when he appeared as the villain

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.880
in Kickboxer: Retaliation.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.860
Sonny Landham made the big time as Billy Sole,
the tracker from the mercenary team slaughtered

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:37.480
in Predator.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.540
He also made appearances in The Warriors,
48 Hours, and many more '80s action flicks.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.340
His acting career dwindled in the mid-'90s
as he devoted more time to his political career,

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:52.620
but it came to a screeching halt in 2008 after
a radio show gone wrong while he was campaigning

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.420
for a Kentucky Senate position on the Libertarian
ticket.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.720
After an increasingly unhinged rant, the Libertarian
Party withdrew their support for Landham's

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.260
candidacy.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.600
After that, he appeared in a couple roles
for low-budget horror films like Disintegration

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:06.780
and Mental Scars.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.600
Landham passed away in August, 2017 of congestive
heart failure.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.940
Name a high-stakes profession and the chances
are Jesse Ventura has dabbled in it at some

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:16.440
point.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.140
A Vietnam War vet, this former Navy SEAL and
Rolling Stones bodyguard made a name for himself

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.739
as a professional wrestler in the early '80s
before making the move to the big screen.

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:29.900
He got to rub shoulders with genre kingpin
Arnold Schwarzenegger on multiple occasions,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.680
perhaps most notably in 1987's Predator.

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:37.020
Ventura played the bullish, tobacco-chewing
Gunner Blain Cooper in the classic film, and

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.900
he almost upstaged the leading man despite
a limited amount of screen time.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:41.660
"You're hit!

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.040
You're bleeding, man."

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.520
"I ain't got time to bleed."

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.020
"Oh.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:47.820
Okay."

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.840
Ventura made another notable appearance in
1993's Demolition Man, but by the end of that

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:57.870
decade he had switched his focus to politics,
becoming the governor of Minnesota from 1999

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.860
to 2003.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.420
Although he started on the New York theater
circuit, Peter Weller eventually headed out

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.420
west in search of movie work, arriving in
Hollywood during the action movie boom.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:12.559
Weller gave viewers a glimpse of what he was
capable of in campy 1984 action satire The

00:04:12.559 --> 00:04:16.780
Adventures of Buckaroo Banzai, and Paul Verhoeven
apparently liked what he saw enough to cast

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.570
Weller in what would become a career-defining
role: Officer Alex Murphy, a.k.a. RoboCop.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:23.500
"What are your prime directives?"

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:25.900
"Serve the public trust.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.280
Protect the innocent.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.480
Uphold the law."

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.380
The film was a success, critically and financially,
and would eventually be regarded as a sci-fi

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.080
movie classic.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.390
Unfortunately for Weller, 1990's RoboCop 2
was a bog-standard action movie, with all

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:43.160
the gore but none of the heart that made the
1987 original a success.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.350
He eventually moved behind the camera for
work, directing several episodes of Sons of

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:51.080
Anarchy and even appearing in front of the
camera for the sixth and seventh seasons.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:56.220
Weller became a literature and fine arts teacher
at New York's Syracuse University for a time,

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.460
putting his master's degree in Renaissance
Art History to good use.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.060
He continues to act though, making appearances
in television shows like The Last Ship and

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:04.560
Forever.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:09.900
Charles Bronson was a no-nonsense tough guy
even before he started his acting career way

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.480
back in the 1950s.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:16.060
Born to Lithuanian immigrants, he fought in
World War II in order to escape the grueling

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.180
conditions at the mine his family worked for,
during which he received a Purple Heart.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.900
After taking theater classes while supporting
himself with odd jobs, Bronson eventually

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.220
moved to Hollywood, where he took on television
roles.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.280
In the '70s, Bronson starred in his most famous
role, vigilante Paul Kersey in the Death Wish

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:32.780
films.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.940
"Do you believe in Jesus?"

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.260
"Yes I do."

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.720
"Well, you're gonna meet him."

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.140
That role served him well through three sequels
in the '80s, earning him a rough-and-tumble

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:48.320
reputation he embellished with tales of his
arrests and violent hobbies, all of which

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.580
he completely made up.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.460
Bronson's gritty archetype grew archaic in
the '90s, but what ultimately ended his career

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:56.260
was hip surgery in 1998.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.060
He struggled with health problems afterwards,
eventually passing away in 2003 at the age

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:01.800
of 81.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.960
Taking over from Roger Moore as James Bond
was never going to be easy, but it was a chance

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.880
that Timothy Dalton grabbed with both hands.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.940
The classically trained actor made his debut
in 1968's The Lion in Winter but had failed

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.340
to climb the ladder from character actor to
star.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.340
Bond was his chance to do just that.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.840
Even though he only played 007 twice, in 1987's
The Living Daylights and then again in 1989's

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.290
Licence to Kill, both of his appearances were
ultimately well-received by the critics.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.720
"Would you get me a medium dry vodka martini?"

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.330
"Why don't you ask-"
"Shaken.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.320
Not stirred."

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.040
A contract dispute between Eon Productions
and MGM delayed Dalton's proposed third outing

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.500
as Bond and gave the actor a legal way out.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:44.360
He decided to call it quits so he wouldn't
be forever remembered as Bond, but that's

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.440
what happened anyway.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.840
Still, he didn't stop acting; he's appeared
in Edgar Wright's Hot Fuzz, had a recurring

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.300
guest role in the tv series Chuck, and starred
as Sir Malcolm Murray in Penny Dreadful.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.320
Michael Biehn owes his stint as an '80s action
star to James Cameron, who cast him in three

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.380
of his earliest films.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:07.620
Biehn played Kyle Reese in 1984's The Terminator,
Hicks in 1986's Aliens, and Coffey in 1989's

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.760
The Abyss, giving him plenty of fans in sci-fi
and horror circles.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.330
The most iconic of those characters wound
up being Kyle Reese, though at the time Biehn

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.080
had no idea just how big The Terminator would
become.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:27.380
"Come with me if you want to live."

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.020
Biehn told Den of Geek that:

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.150
"People talk about it being an iconic movie,
with iconic characters and so on [but] it

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.830
was never that back then, and you have to
remember, too, that Arnold was not a superstar.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:40.400
He was basically a bodybuilder and Mr. Universe."

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:45.180
Schwarzenegger went on to cement his action
hero status with 1987's Predator, but for

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.320
Biehn, that star-making role never came.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.640
His most noteworthy appearances since have
been in 1996's The Rock and 2007's Planet

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:53.140
Terror.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:57.560
Taking part in Planet Terror rekindled a love
for B-movies that Biehn forgot he had and

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.080
inspired him to start his own production company
specializing in grindhouse-style features.

